  "limitations": [
    "Tiny benchmark: 12 synthetic candidates × 30 synthetic postings (360 pairs). Results are indicative only and confidence intervals are wide.",
    "Labels were assigned by the project author with a written rubric; there was no second annotator, so inter-annotator agreement is unknown, and the author also designed the scorer (risk of shared assumptions).",
    "Candidates and postings were written to cover distinct fields, which makes field discrimination easier than in real data. Real postings are noisier and longer.",
    "The skill taxonomy was written by the same author; extraction recall on skills outside the taxonomy is zero by construction (a few such skills are included in the gold annotations to keep this visible).",
    "Default scoring weights come from the project brief and were not tuned; no supervised ranker was trained because no genuine labelled training data exists.",
    "This evaluates ranking agreement with relevance labels, not employment outcomes. No claim is made that CareerMatch AI improves hiring results."
  ]
